  "interpretation": {
    "result": "Neumaier compensation-state merge reproduced the correctly rounded reference in all 192 measured runs. Scalar Neumaier was correct in 168/192 runs; naive scalar was correct in 0/192.",
    "performance": "No threaded configuration exceeded its matching serial baseline in any of the 192 configurations. Free-threaded mode showed process CPU/wall ratio above 1.05 in 72/96 configurations, while GIL-enabled mode did so in 0/96.",
    "runner_comparison": "Run 1 used the same AWS x86-64 runner family for GIL and free-threaded modes. Run 2 used AWS for free-threaded and Azure for GIL-enabled mode; run 2 is not a matched cross-mode comparison.",
    "memory": "tracemalloc records Python allocation peaks only; it is not process RSS or native allocator memory."
  },
  "limitations": [
    "Hosted x86-64 GitHub runners only; no user-owned physical workstation, ARM64 host, or independently administered machine was tested.",
    "Three measured repetitions per configuration and one warm-up; medians are exploratory and not a robust performance distribution.",
    "The synthetic 196,608-value cancellation workloads may not represent production application workloads.",
    "No configuration achieved wall-time speedup over its matching serial baseline, despite evidence of aggregate multi-thread CPU use in free-threaded runs.",
    "tracemalloc is Python allocation accounting only; RSS, native allocator memory, DRAM bandwidth, CPU frequency, context switches and thermal state were not measured."
  ]
